    "lawCode": "Amd §6, Chap 514 of 1983",
    "lawSection": "Private Housing Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A7087A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend chapter 514 of the laws of 1983 amending\nthe  private  housing finance law and the real property tax law relating\nto the powers of the New York city housing development  corporation  and\nthe New York state housing finance agency to finance certain multi-fami-\nly housing, in relation to the effectiveness of such chapter\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis  proposed legislation would extend the sunset provision of subdivi-\nsion 23-c of Section 654 of Article 12 of the  Private  Housing  Finance\nLaw, which establishes the New York City Housing Development Corporation\n(\"HDC\").  The  sunset  provision currently calls for subdivision 23-c to\nexpire on June 30, 2009. This bill would preserve  subdivision  23-c  in\nfull  force  and  effect  until  June  30, 2011, thereby allowing HDC to\ncontinue to issue notes and bonds for the financing of housing  develop-\nments.\n\nREASONS FOR SUPPORT:\n\nEnactment  of  this  proposed legislation would allow HDC to continue to\nassist in the financing of newly constructed and  rehabilitated  low-and\nmoderate-income housing in the City of New York. The bill would continue\nHDC's authorization to enter into direct loan agreements with developers\nof low- and moderate-income housing, as well as joint financing programs\nwith  recognized  lending  institutions.  Pursuant  to a joint financing\nprogram, HDC lends funds obtained from the sale of its securities,  both\ntax-exempt and taxable, to financial institutions, which in turn re-lend\nthe  proceeds  to private developments for the construction or rehabili-\ntation of low- or moderate-income housing. In cases of conduit  lending,\nthe responsibilities of underwriting and servicing the loans are assumed\nby  the lending institutions, which also bear the risks of default under\nthe mortgages. HDC does not rely upon the credit (or tax levy) of either\nthe City or the State of New York to pay bondholders.\n\nSince its creation in 1971, HDC has financed the construction or preser-\nvation of more than 135,000 units of  affordable  housing  in  New  York\nCity.  More  than 75,000 units have been financed since 1984 pursuant to\nsubdivision 23-c of Section 654. Overall,  HDC's  portfolio  includes  a\nbroad  array  of  housing,  from  low-income  tax credit developments to\nmixed-income developments and aging developments originally financed  in\nconjunction  with  direct  federal subsidies. The vast majority of units\nfinanced by HDC are income restricted to be affordable to  families  and\nindividuals who earn from 40% to 130% of the area median income.\n\nUnder  Mayor  Bloomberg's expanded \"New Housing Marketplace\" initiative,\nHDC is responsible for creating or preserving 42,000  units  of  housing\nduring an eleven year period ending in June 2014. As of January 1, 2009,\nHDC  has  financed  the construction or preservation of more than 40,000\napartments under the plan, and HDC expects to exceed its goal  in  2009.\nThe  housing  units  for  the Mayor's plan are financed under three main\n\nprograms: (1) Low Income Affordable Marketplace Program  (\"LAMP\")  which\ncombines  tax-exempt  bonds  and  4%  as-of-right low income housing tax\ncredits (\"LIHTC\") to produce housing affordable to people  earning  less\nthan  60%  of  area  median  income, (2) New Housing Opportunity Program\n(\"New HOP\"), a taxable bond program used to finance rental units afford-\nable to middle-income New Yorkers, and (3) Preservation,  including  its\nMitchell-Lama  restructuring  program  (described  below) as well as its\nprogram  for  preserving  federally  assisted  developments   originally\nfinanced under Sections 8, 202 and 236 of the National Housing Act.\n\nIn  June  of  2004  Mayor  Bloomberg announced a voluntary Mitchell-Lama\nrestructuring program along with a rehabilitation loan program  for  all\nMitchell-Lama  projects  with first or second mortgages that are held by\nHDC or the City. The program provides for refinancing  or  restructuring\nof the Mitchell-Lama's existing mortgage in exchange for a commitment to\nremaining  the  program  for  a  minimum  of an additional 15 years. The\nresponse to this program has been significant. To date HDC has  restruc-\ntured  38  projects  with more than 18,250 units preserved. HDC is still\nactively working with Mitchell-Lamas in New York  City  and  expects  to\nprovide funds to preserve even more units over the next few years.\n\nOverall, in 2009, HDC expects to close on financing or refinancing tran-\nsactions for over 25 projects involving the construction or preservation\nof approximately 5,000 units of housing.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: New Legislation.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None to the State.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
